Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent visual magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) are often closer or intrinsically luminous; giants tend to be intrinsically luminous but can appear faint if far away. Alone, Vmag is not a strong indicator of giant status.
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer distance; giants can be at various distances, but small parallax (farther stars) could be giants due to their brightness.
- e_Plx (error in parallax): High error reduces reliability of distance estimate; less useful directly for classification.
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature; giants often have specific color ranges depending on spectral type; e.g., giants tend to be redder (higher B-V) than main sequence stars of same spectral class.
- SpType (spectral type): The luminosity class in spectral type (e.g., III, II) directly indicates giant status; V means main sequence (not giant), III or II means giant or bright giant.
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants have lower (brighter) absolute magnitudes than dwarfs; higher Amag values (fainter) usually mean dwarfs, lower Amag (brighter) means giants.

Step 2. Based on the above and examples, infer 10 conditions per class.

10 different conditions for class "0" (not giant):

- SpType is in [G1V, F3V, K5V, F5/F6V, F0V, F7V, K7V, K1IV/V, G8IV, F8V] (main sequence or subgiant not clearly giant)
- Amag (absolute magnitude) >= 15
- Plx >= 5 (closer stars, often dwarfs)
- B-V <= 1.0 (bluer or moderately colored stars typical of dwarfs)
- Vmag >= 6.5 (fainter apparent magnitude, often dwarfs at moderate distance)
- SpType contains "V" but not "III" or "II" (main sequence stars)
- Amag is within range of [15, 23]
- Plx is within range of [5, 30]
- e_Plx <= 2.5 (more reliable parallax, typical for closer dwarfs)
- B-V is within range of [0.3, 1.0]

10 different conditions for class "1" (giant):

- SpType is in [G8III, G2III, K0III, K1IIICN, M1II, B0.5Ia, G8III/IV] (luminosity class III or II or Ia, giants or bright giants)
- Amag < 15 (brighter absolute magnitude typical of giants)
- Plx <= 5 (farther stars, consistent with giants)
- B-V >= 1.0 (redder stars typical for giants)
- Vmag <= 7.0 (brighter apparent magnitude, often giants)
- SpType contains "III" or "II" or "Ia" (giant or bright giant luminosity class)
- Amag is within range of [10, 15]
- Plx is within range of [0, 5]
- e_Plx <= 1.5 (reasonable parallax error for distant giants)
- B-V is within range of [1.0, 1.6]

These conditions align with the given examples and general astrophysical knowledge about giants versus dwarfs.

